  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through Friday)...\n\nThe main focus through the next few days continues to be the very\nhot conditions as a strong ridge of high pressure builds over the\nregion...with very warm temperatures aloft (700mb temperatures near \nor exceeding 20C and 850 mb temperatures of 35-40C). We expect\nthis to result in temperatures of 100 to 112 degrees, warmest on \nSunday. Lower river valley locations east of Billings continue to\nhave the best chance at exceeding 110 degrees. Temperatures this \nwarm will threaten all-time record high temperatures at all \nclimate sites Sunday. Along with the hot daytime temperatures, \novernight low temperatures in the 60s and 70s, warmest Sunday \nnight, will exacerbate the risk of heat stress. A weak front will \nsweep into the area Sunday evening from the west. This will cool \ntemps 5 to 10 degrees for Monday (relative to Sunday), but highs \nwill still be in the upper 90s to around 100 degrees...and the \novernight lows Sunday night will be quite mild ranging from 60s to\nnear 80 degrees. An Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect \nthrough Monday.\n\nVery dry conditions are still expected to accompany the heat, \nwith relative humidity values decreasing into the single digits to\nteens percent later today and again Sunday. Breezy south to \nsouthwest winds develop over the area (gusts 20s to 30s mph) \nduring the day on Sunday, ahead of a cold front from the northwest\nSunday evening/night. The combination of breezy winds, very hot \ntemperatures, and very dry conditions will lead to elevated to \ncritical fire weather concerns. We have upgraded Sunday to a Red\nFlag Warning for much of the area. Please take care not to cause \na spark or do any burning. \n\nEnsembles continue to shift the ridge east a bit by midweek \nallowing temperatures to trend a little cooler (highs in the \nupper 80s and 90s). Models indicate monsoonal moisture will slide \ninto our region from the southwest with hints of occasional weak \nperturbations in the mean flow. This means a daily chance of \nshowers/thunderstorms for the area along with higher relative \nhumidity values. Ensembles continue to suggest remarkable PWATs \npossibly reaching up to 1.50 inches in the area from Tuesday \nonward. So any decent impulse in the monsoonal flow may result in \nstorms with locally heavy rainfall. We will need to monitor those\nPWAT trends closely heading into next week. \n\nBelow are the all-time record high temperatures at each of our \nclimate stations:\n\nBillings, MT    108F\nLivingston, MT  105F\nMiles City, MT  111F\nSheridan, WY    107F\nBaker, MT       109F\n\nBT",
